course: biology
lesson: 247
title: Defences: poison, warning colours and mimics
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A poisonous animal does not bite you; it harms whoever eats it or touches it, so it is best for it if nobody tries in the first place.
explain: That is why many poisonous animals are dressed in red, yellow, orange or black, nature's danger signs, like the yellow and black tape round a hole in the road.
explain: A tiny bright blue frog in the zoo rainforest house is saying «don't eat me», and a bird that once ate a bitter orange butterfly remembers the colours and leaves the next one alone.
explain: Some harmless animals cheat: a hoverfly on your ice cream wears wasp stripes but cannot sting at all; copying a dangerous animal like this is called mimicry.
explain: Many poisonous animals do not make their poison themselves: they get it from what they eat.
fact: Poison dart frogs have poison in their skin and bright colours that warn predators; in the wild they get the poison from ants and mites they eat, so zoo frogs fed on crickets are not poisonous.
fact: The golden poison frog of Colombia carries enough poison to kill about ten people, and some peoples there wiped blowgun darts on its back for hunting, without hurting the frog, which is how the frogs got their name.
fact: Monarch caterpillars eat milkweed, keep its poison in their bodies, and birds that eat a monarch get sick and learn to avoid orange and black butterflies; the viceroy looks very similar and also tastes bad, so they share the warning.
fact: Pufferfish, called fugu in Japan, have a very strong poison in their liver and other parts, made by bacteria in their food; only chefs with a special licence may prepare fugu, and farm-raised pufferfish can be poison-free.
fact: Ladybirds bleed a bitter, smelly yellow liquid from their knees when attacked, called reflex bleeding, and their red and black colours warn birds; their spots show the kind of ladybird, not its age.
fact: Cane toads have poison glands behind their heads and can make dogs very sick; they were brought to Australia in 1935 to eat beetles in sugar cane fields, spread, poisoned native animals, and now number in the hundreds of millions, as was reported.
fact: The hoverfly is harmless but has wasp-like yellow and black stripes, which is mimicry; hoverflies can hover still in the air and have two wings, while wasps have four.
fact: You do not get warts from touching a toad, because warts come from a human virus, but toads have bumpy glands with bad-tasting stuff, so wash your hands after holding one.
fact: Toads walk more than they hop and have drier, bumpier skin than frogs.
fact: The hooded pitohui of New Guinea is one of very few poisonous birds, with poison in its feathers and skin from beetles it eats; scientists found out when handling the birds made their fingers numb.
fact: Hedgehogs sometimes chew bad-tasting things like toad skin and spread the frothy spit on their spines, called self-anointing, and nobody knows exactly why.
fact: Red, yellow, orange and black often mean danger in nature, predators learn quickly or are born wary of them, and people copy nature with yellow and black warning signs.
exercise: Why are poison dart frogs so brightly coloured? | To warn predators «don't eat me» | To attract flies for it to eat | To keep cool in the hot sun | To hide among bright flowers and leaves
exercise: Where do wild poison dart frogs get their poison? | From the ants and mites they eat | They make it from sunlight on their skin | From the dirty water they sit in all day | From the other frogs they fight with
exercise: Why are poison frogs in a zoo usually not poisonous? | They eat crickets instead of their wild food | Zookeepers wash all the poison off their skin every day | They lose it in the cold | They are a different kind of frog
exercise: How did poison dart frogs get their name? | Hunters wiped blowgun darts on their backs | They shoot little poison darts from their long tongues | They are shaped like darts | They jump as fast as a dart
exercise: Why do birds learn to leave monarch butterflies alone? | A monarch is poisonous from milkweed and makes them sick | Monarchs fly far too fast for birds to catch | Monarchs bite birds hard on the beak when they are grabbed | Monarchs are too small to bother with
exercise: Why does the viceroy look so much like the monarch? | Both taste bad, so they share the same warning | They are the same butterfly in two coats | The viceroy is really just the monarch's baby, before it grows | Birds paint them both orange
exercise: Who may prepare pufferfish to eat in Japan? | Only chefs with a special licence | Any cook at home with a sharp knife | Only the fishermen out on the boat | Nobody, it is never eaten in Japan
exercise: Where does a pufferfish's poison come from? | Bacteria in its food | Its fins in the sun | The salt in the sea | It is born with it whatever it eats
exercise: What is the yellow drop a ladybird leaves on your finger? | Bitter liquid bled from its knees | Its honey, stored up for the winter | Its eggs | Water it drank from the pond
exercise: What do a ladybird's spots tell you? | What kind of ladybird it is | How old it is in years and months | How many babies it has had so far | How many more days it will live
exercise: Why were cane toads brought to Australia in 1935? | To eat beetles in sugar cane fields | As pets for children to keep at home | To clean up the rivers and streams of algae | For people to cook and eat at big feasts
exercise: What is mimicry? | A harmless animal copying a dangerous one | An animal hiding under the ground from danger | An animal changing its food in the winter | An animal sleeping all winter
exercise: How many wings does a hoverfly have? | Two | Four | Six of them | None at all
exercise: Do you get warts from touching a toad? | No, warts come from a human virus | Yes, every time you touch one | Only from green toads in ponds | Only if you touch it twice
exercise: What makes the hooded pitohui unusual? | It is one of very few poisonous birds | It is the only bird that cannot fly | It lives under water | It has no feathers
exercise: What do hedgehogs do in self-anointing? | Spread frothy spit on their spines | Roll in puddles to get their spines clean | Dig a nest of leaves for the winter | Bite off their old spines to grow new ones
exercise: Which colours often mean danger in nature? | Red, yellow, orange and black | Green and brown | Pale blue and pink | White and grey
test: In a cartoon a witch says touching a toad gives you warts. What is right? | Warts come from a human virus, but wash your hands after holding a toad | It is true, toads give you warts at once, wherever they touch your skin | It is true, but only on Fridays | Warts come from frogs but never from toads
test: «argh a wasp on my ice cream!! oh wait it's just hovering still» What could it be? | A harmless hoverfly in wasp colours | A baby wasp that cannot sting | A bee pretending to be a wasp | A moth in the sunshine
test: «3 wasps and 2 hoverflies on the jam jar. how many wings, go» | 16 | 10 | 20 | 12
test: «5 ladybirds on my arm. legs?» | 30 | 20 | 40 | 25
test: «my mate says the more spots a ladybird's got the older it is, it's got 7 so it's 7» What is right? | No, the spots show the kind of ladybird, not its age | Yes, one spot for each year, so a big spotty one is very old | Yes, one spot for each month | No, spots show how many babies it has
test: What would happen if you fed a zoo poison frog only crickets for its whole life? | It would not become poisonous | It would become twice as poisonous | It would turn green | It would stop eating
test: «our dog bit a cane toad and he's drooling, he'll be grand» What is right? | Cane toad poison can make dogs very sick, so get a vet quickly | Toads are harmless to dogs | Drooling means the dog liked the taste and wants another one | Give the dog the rest of the toad
test: A young bird eats an orange and black butterfly and is sick. What will it probably do next time? | Avoid orange and black butterflies | Eat two at once to get it over with | Only eat them at night | Feed them to its chicks
test: Why are danger signs and warning tape yellow and black? | People copied nature's warning colours | They are the cheapest paints | It was chosen by a coin toss | Because those colours glow in the dark
test: Scientists' fingers went numb after handling a bird in New Guinea. What had they found? | A poisonous bird, the hooded pitohui | A bird with electric feathers | A bird covered in ice | A bird coated all over in sticky glue from trees
test: «that hedgehog chewed a toad and now he's frothing, is he mad» What is going on? | It is self-anointing, spreading the frothy spit on its spines | It has rabies for sure | It is laughing at the toad | It is blowing bubbles to call its friends over for a toad feast
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of bright coloration in poisonous animals? | It serves as a warning to predators that the animal is unpalatable or harmful | It serves exclusively to attract mates in darkness, the colours glowing at night | It provides concealment amongst foliage | It regulates the temperature of the body
exam: Identify the principal source from which wild poison dart frogs obtain their toxins. | The ants and mites which constitute their diet | Their own production independent of diet | The water of the streams wherein they dwell | Contact with poisonous plants upon which they rest
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the hoverfly. | It is harmless, its resemblance to the wasp constituting an instance of mimicry | It is equipped with a sting more potent than that of the wasp, and stings without warning | It is a juvenile form of the wasp | It possesses four wings in the manner of the wasp
exam: Ascertain the reason for which cane toads were introduced into Australia in 1935. | The control of beetles in the sugar cane fields | The provision of food for the population | The supply of pets to households | The purification of rivers
